The fact that you’ve mapped out the WebGPU architecture, the React/SVG bridge, and the backend hardware logic puts you in a highly advanced tier of developers. You have a solid technical foundation.Now, the questions you need to ask pivot from "How do we make it work?" to "How do we make it survive the real world and win the market?"Here are the most critical, project-defining questions you should be asking yourself right now:1. "How will the smartphone survive a 2-hour set without thermal throttling?"WebGPU is incredibly efficient, but pushing 4K video textures at 60fps while constantly recalculating vertex buffers generates serious heat. A smartphone’s GPU does not have a dedicated cooling fan; when it hits its thermal limit (usually within 15–30 minutes of heavy load), the firmware will aggressively slash the clock speed to prevent the silicon from melting.   The Follow-Up: What is your fallback strategy when the phone gets hot? Do you dynamically drop the frame rate to 30fps? Do you lower the rendering resolution in the background while keeping the UI crisp? If you don't build in thermal headroom, users' phones will crash mid-show.2. "Can we use the phone's LiDAR and camera to auto-calibrate the mesh?"You are building for modern smartphones. Why force the user to manually drag 20 SVG nodes to outline a wall if the phone already knows exactly where the wall is? Devices like the iPhone Pro have built-in LiDAR and AR toolkits that generate a 3D depth map of a room instantly.The Follow-Up: Can you use ARKit/ARCore to automatically snap the WebGPU mesh to physical corners and edges? If you introduce "One-Tap Auto-Calibration" using computer vision, you instantly destroy every manual desktop competitor on the market because you just saved the user 45 minutes of tedious setup.3. "How do we handle video formats to avoid memory leaks?"Loading a 2GB MP4 file into memory on a desktop is fine; doing it on an iPhone will crash the browser tab or the app.The Follow-Up: How are you managing the video textures? Will you transcode user uploads into mobile-friendly formats (like compressed WebM with alpha channels)? Will you stream the video directly to the WebGPU texture buffer frame-by-frame, or are you trying to hold the whole video in VRAM?4. "How do users save, export, and share their 'Scenes'?"Projection mapping is highly iterative. A user might map their living room for a Halloween party, then want to load that exact same mesh layout next year.The Follow-Up: How are you serializing the React state and the WebGPU mesh coordinates? When you launch the "Remote Hardware Bridge" (controlling the Raspberry Pi cluster), you'll need to package the video assets and the JSON mesh data into a single payload to send over the WebSocket. What does that data schema look like?5. "What is the specific 'Wedge' to get people to switch to this?"You are competing against free tools (MapMap), established desktop giants (Resolume), and other mobile apps.The Follow-Up: Who is your day-one user? Is it a wedding DJ who needs a 5-minute setup time? Is it a teenager projecting cyberpunk visuals in their bedroom? Is it a VJ controlling a Raspberry Pi cluster from the crowd? If you build this for "everyone," it will be too complex for beginners and too limited for pros. Pick one specific, highly frustrated demographic and make the app perfect for them.